# Every performance number Mira publishes, once.
#
# A figure quoted in four files is a figure that will be right in three of them
# after the next run. `xtask drift` already solved that shape for the crate count
# and the binary size; this file is the same idea for everything a load test
# produces. One entry per quantity, the sites that quote it listed beside it, and
# `cargo run -p xtask -- measurements` failing the build when a site disagrees.
#
# Each entry has five parts and all five are load-bearing:
#
#   key         a name a harness can emit and this file can be keyed by.
#   value       the measured number. This is the variable. Everything else in
#               the tree is a rendering of it.
#   unit        what the number counts.
#   measures    what the quantity *is* — the numerator, the denominator, and
#               what sits inside the measurement and outside it. A number
#               without this cannot be compared with anyone else's, which is the
#               whole failure mode docs/internals/measurement.md exists to stop.
#   provenance  the command that produces it. `unscripted` where none does, and
#               that is a gap named rather than hidden.
#   quoted      how prose spells it, and where. Several renderings per value is
#               normal — e2e.md prints 886,147 and the README rounds to 886k —
#               and every one of them is checked.
#
# To re-measure:
#
#   make build && scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
#   make measurements-ingest RUN=/tmp/mira-sweep/run.json
#
# The sweep appends one JSON object per pass; the ingest takes the median per key
# across them and reports every value that moved and every site that now lies.
# `WRITE=1` updates the values here. It deliberately does not rewrite the prose,
# because the sentence around a number is a claim ("worth between a quarter and
# two fifths") that a new number can invalidate in a way no formatter can see.

# The box every figure below was taken on. A measurement without one is a
# measurement of nothing: docs/internals/measurement.md#the-box says why this
# machine's noise floor is part of the reading.
box:
  machine: Apple M3 Pro, 12 cores, 18 GiB
  os: macOS, aarch64-apple-darwin
  build: release profile, --locked, generator co-resident with the server

# The corpus the section 11 query rows were read from. It came off a `--for` run,
# so these bytes are reproducible in method rather than byte for byte; the
# argument that rebuilds the same shape is `--records 54172384 --conns 4
# --batch 8192`, which is 826 rounds per connection of 2 x 8192 + 12 records, and
# it is what conn-sweep.sh runs. The first sweep through it replaces the figures
# below and closes this gap.
corpus:
  logs: 27066368
  spans: 27066368
  points: 39648
  bytes: 8944889856
  tables: 1652
  blocks: 316

measurements:

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Ingest. One connection sweep produces every row here; the shapes are the
  # ones the docs quote, not every shape the sweep runs.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - key: ingest.records_per_s.conns1
    value: 629384
    unit: records/s
    measures: >
      Records the server acknowledged, divided by the harness's wall clock.
      A record is one log record, one span or one metric data point — not one
      export and not one byte. Acknowledged means the 200 came back, which under
      ack-after-durability means the bytes are in the log. Shed exports are not
      counted and the run asserts there were none.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "629,384"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, docs/internals/e2e.md]

  - key: ingest.records_per_s.conns4
    value: 1350502
    unit: records/s
    measures: >
      As ingest.records_per_s.conns1, at four writer connections — the shape
      quoted for a paired comparison because it is the one that reproduces, 10%
      across three passes against 39% at ninety-six.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "1,350,502"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, docs/internals/e2e.md]

  - key: ingest.records_per_s.conns32
    value: 1537875
    unit: records/s
    measures: >
      As ingest.records_per_s.conns1, at thirty-two. The peak of a plateau
      rather than a peak: sixteen and thirty-two are within 2% of each other on
      medians whose passes span 6% and 16%.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "1,537,875"
        sites:
          [
            README.md,
            docs/architecture.md,
            docs/market.md,
            docs/internals/e2e.md,
          ]

  - key: ingest.records_per_s.conns96
    value: 1136941
    unit: records/s
    measures: >
      As ingest.records_per_s.conns1, at ninety-six — past the plateau, where
      added connections buy waiters on one log mutex rather than appends.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "1,136,941"
        sites:
          [
            docs/architecture.md,
            docs/market.md,
            docs/internals/e2e.md,
            CHANGELOG.md,
          ]

  - key: ingest.cores.conns1
    value: 0.71
    unit: cores
    measures: >
      CPU-seconds the *server* process burned during the run, divided by wall
      clock. A meter reading, not a core count somebody chose: the harness takes
      `ps -o cputime=` either side of the run and differences it. The generator's
      own CPU is not in it, and the generator is on the same twelve cores.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "0.71"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, docs/internals/e2e.md]

  - key: ingest.cores.conns4
    value: 1.75
    unit: cores
    measures: As ingest.cores.conns1, at four connections.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "1.75"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, docs/internals/e2e.md]

  - key: ingest.cores.conns32
    value: 2.23
    unit: cores
    measures: >
      As ingest.cores.conns1, at thirty-two — and, to two decimals, the same at
      ninety-six, which is the observation the plateau diagnosis rests on: ten of
      twelve cores idle at both ends of a 26% throughput fall.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "2.23"
        sites:
          [
            README.md,
            docs/architecture.md,
            docs/market.md,
            docs/internals/e2e.md,
          ]

  - key: ingest.per_core.conns1
    value: 886147
    unit: records/s/core
    measures: >
      ingest.records_per_s.conns1 divided by ingest.cores.conns1. The comparable
      figure: an aggregate rate is a property of the offered load and moves with
      `--conns` without a line of the server changing. Highest at one connection,
      where there is nothing to contend over, so it is a ceiling and not an
      operating point.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "886,147"
        sites: [docs/internals/e2e.md]
      - as: "886k"
        sites:
          [
            README.md,
            docs/architecture.md,
            docs/market.md,
            docs/internals/e2e.md,
          ]

  - key: ingest.per_core.conns96
    value: 515288
    unit: records/s/core
    measures: As ingest.per_core.conns1, at ninety-six connections.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "515,288"
        sites: [docs/internals/e2e.md]

  - key: ingest.wire_mib_s.conns4
    value: 176.5
    unit: MiB/s
    measures: >
      Uncompressed OTLP protobuf written to the socket, including request
      headers, divided by wall clock. Not bytes on disk and not a compressed
      figure — the comparison peers publish a wire rate, so this is the one that
      can sit beside theirs. At four connections Mira's record averages 137 B.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "176.5"
        sites: [docs/market.md, docs/internals/e2e.md]

  - key: ingest.ack_p50_ms.conns4
    value: 8.5
    unit: ms
    measures: >
      Milliseconds from the first byte of an export written to the socket to the
      200 read back, p50 over every export of the run. With `ingest.wal` on — the
      default — that is a `write(2)` into the page cache and not a block seal.
      Client-side, so it includes the loopback round trip.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "8.5 ms"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, docs/internals/e2e.md]

  - key: ingest.ack_p99_ms.conns4
    value: 55
    unit: ms
    measures: As ingest.ack_p50_ms.conns4, at p99.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "55 ms"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, docs/internals/e2e.md]

  - key: ingest.ack_p99_ms.conns96
    value: 2661
    unit: ms
    measures: >
      As ingest.ack_p99_ms.conns4, at ninety-six — eighteen times the four-
      connection figure for 14% more throughput, which is what the plateau costs.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "2,661"
        sites: [docs/market.md, docs/internals/e2e.md]
      - as: "2.7 s"
        sites: [docs/market.md]

  - key: ingest.ack_p50_ms.logoff
    value: 657
    unit: ms
    measures: >
      As ingest.ack_p50_ms.conns4 but with `ingest.wal` off, where the
      acknowledgement waits for the block seal rather than a page-cache write.
      A different quantity from the row above, not a worse measurement of the
      same one.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "657"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, CHANGELOG.md]

  - key: ingest.ack_p99_ms.logoff
    value: 2647
    unit: ms
    measures: As ingest.ack_p50_ms.logoff, at p99.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "2,647"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md]

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Resident footprint. Sampled from outside, because Mira reads through mmap
  # and a heap counter would report a flattering number.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - key: rss_mib.conns1
    value: 232
    unit: MiB
    measures: >
      Peak resident set of the server process over the run, `ps -o rss=` at
      4 Hz. Whole process: both UIs, the query path and every mapped block page
      a query touched. Uncapped — no memory cgroup, unlike every peer figure it
      is quoted against.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "232 MiB"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, docs/internals/e2e.md]

  - key: rss_mib.conns4
    value: 689
    unit: MiB
    measures: As rss_mib.conns1, at four connections.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "689 MiB"
        sites:
          [
            docs/architecture.md,
            docs/market.md,
            docs/internals/e2e.md,
            CHANGELOG.md,
          ]

  - key: rss_mib.conns96
    value: 1648
    unit: MiB
    measures: >
      As rss_mib.conns1, at ninety-six. RSS is not flat in connection count and
      this row is why the headline footprint figure is labelled with its shape.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "1,648 MiB"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, docs/internals/e2e.md]

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Query. The section 11 table, read back from the corpus above after a
  # restart. "First" is the first call into a fresh process; "steady" is the
  # same call repeated. No script rebuilds these — see the gap below.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - key: query.attr_absent.steady_ms
    value: 2.6
    unit: ms
    measures: >
      The server's own `elapsed_us`, not a client round trip: time inside the
      query handler for an attribute equality that matches nothing anywhere in
      the store. Zero of 137 blocks opened — the Bloom sidecar answers it — so
      this prices the pruning path and nothing else.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "2.6 ms"
        sites: [README.md, docs/architecture.md]

  - key: query.attr_absent.first_ms
    value: 8.3
    unit: ms
    measures: >
      As query.attr_absent.steady_ms, on the first call after a process restart.
      The gap between first and steady is virtual-memory work — establishing 316
      blocks' worth of mappings and faulting them in.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "8.3 ms"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md]

  - key: query.attr_match.first_ms
    value: 4.1
    unit: ms
    measures: >
      As query.attr_absent.first_ms, for an attribute equality that does match:
      1 of 137 blocks opened, 204,800 rows scanned.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "4.1 ms"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md]

  - key: query.unfiltered_100.first_ms
    value: 29.1
    unit: ms
    measures: >
      As query.attr_absent.first_ms, for `limit 100` with no predicate. One
      block, 204,800 rows — the cost is the block open, not the limit.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "29.1"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md]

  - key: query.trace_by_id.steady_ms
    value: 4.7
    unit: ms
    measures: >
      As query.attr_absent.steady_ms, for every span of one trace id out of
      27.1M spans on disk. A trace id carries no time bound, so every block is a
      candidate and only `trace.idx` prunes: 2 of 155 blocks opened, 73,728 rows.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "4.7 ms"
        sites: [README.md, docs/architecture.md, docs/agents.md]

  - key: query.metric_names.first_ms
    value: 8.9
    unit: ms
    measures: >
      As query.attr_absent.first_ms, for the metric-name catalogue: 24 of 24
      blocks, because every metric block can hold a name no other one does.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "8.9 ms"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md]

  - key: query.substring_scan.steady_ms
    value: 885
    unit: ms
    measures: >
      As query.attr_absent.steady_ms, for a substring with no time bound over
      27,066,368 rows and 137 of 137 blocks — the unprunable case, and the only
      row of the table that is not warm: 8.33 GiB does not stay resident in 18
      GiB shared with a VM, Docker and a browser.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "885 ms"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, CHANGELOG.md]

  - key: query.substring_scan.first_ms
    value: 1441
    unit: ms
    measures: As query.substring_scan.steady_ms, on the first call after a restart.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "1,441"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md]

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Cost per GB, and the durability floor under the ack.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - key: cost.hot_bytes_per_byte
    value: 1.20
    unit: B/B
    measures: >
      Bytes the data directory grew during the run, divided by uncompressed OTLP
      protobuf bytes the harness sent. Above 1.0 because a hot block carries
      sidecars and is not yet compressed. A delta on both sides: a total over a
      store that already had blocks in it would be meaningless.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "1.20"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md]

  - key: cost.compacted_bytes_per_byte
    value: 0.14
    unit: B/B
    measures: >
      As cost.hot_bytes_per_byte, once the cold tier has rewritten the block
      ZSTD-compressed. The figure to quote for retention sizing; the hot one is
      the transient.
    provenance: scripts/measure/offload-cycle.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "0.14"
        sites:
          [
            README.md,
            docs/architecture.md,
            docs/market.md,
            docs/internals/e2e.md,
          ]

  - key: wal.append_p50_us
    value: 7
    unit: us
    measures: >
      p50 of one durable log append for a 4 KiB body, measured inside the
      process. It is a buffered `write(2)` plus the fixed per-append work, not an
      fsync — the fsync is the sweep's, four times a second, and is
      fsync.full_us below.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "7 µs"
        sites: [README.md, docs/architecture.md, CHANGELOG.md]

  - key: fsync.full_us
    value: 4230
    unit: us
    measures: >
      `File::sync_all()` on this volume, which on macOS is `F_FULLFSYNC` — a
      real platter-or-flash barrier, not a page-cache flush. Quoted as the cost
      the log sweep pays 4 Hz, a ~2% duty cycle on a 250 ms period.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "4,230"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md]

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # What a log fix is worth. section 11 diagnosed the ingest plateau as one mutex
  # held across a `write(2)` and named two fixes; these are the numbers that
  # rejected both. They are ratios rather than rates on purpose — each is taken
  # inside one sitting with both arms in every pass, so the box's 23% day-to-day
  # drift cancels instead of being measured. A ratio here without an `agreed`
  # line in its `measures` is one whose per-pass signs split, which under
  # docs/internals/measurement.md section 3 means it is recorded as not quotable
  # rather than quoted.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - key: wal.ramdisk.records_per_s_ratio.conns32
    value: 1.096
    unit: ratio
    measures: >
      Records/s with `<data-dir>/.wal` symlinked at an APFS RAM disk, over the
      same binary logging to the data volume, at thirty-two connections. Median
      of three per-pass ratios, both arms in every pass, B first; agreed, 3 of 3.
      This is the *envelope* for any change to the log's critical section rather
      than a fix: the RAM disk deletes the section instead of shortening it, so
      no group commit and no log split can be worth more than this.
    provenance: scripts/measure/wal-volume.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "1.096x"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, crates/mira-core/src/wal.rs]

  - key: wal.ramdisk.records_per_s_ratio.conns96
    value: 1.005
    unit: ratio
    measures: >
      As wal.ramdisk.records_per_s_ratio.conns32, at ninety-six. Signs split, 2
      of 3, so the median is recorded and deliberately not quoted as an effect —
      it is quoted as the absence of one.
    provenance: scripts/measure/wal-volume.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "1.005x"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, crates/mira-core/src/wal.rs]

  - key: wal.ramdisk.submit_admit_share
    value: 92
    unit: percent
    measures: >
      `submit.admit` as a share of `submit.total` in the RAM-disk dump at
      thirty-two connections — 44.054 ms of 47.910 ms. The same probe reads
      0.000-0.002 ms in every disk-backed dump in the section 11 table, where it
      was cited as ruling admission out. With the log's write removed the queue
      re-forms here, which is what moves the named constraint off the log and on
      to the flusher.
    provenance: scripts/measure/wal-volume.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "92%"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, crates/mira-core/src/wal.rs]

  - key: wal.split.write_ratio.conns32
    value: 1.943
    unit: ratio
    measures: >
      Mean `wal.write` with one log per signal over the single-log binary it
      replaces, at thirty-two connections. Median of nine per-pass ratios across
      three sittings; agreed, 9 of 9. Three appenders on one volume do not write
      in the time one did, which is why the split gives back in device time what
      it takes off the mutex.
    provenance: scripts/measure/wal-split-ab.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "1.94x"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, crates/mira-core/src/wal.rs]

  - key: wal.split.write_ratio.conns96
    value: 2.394
    unit: ratio
    measures: As wal.split.write_ratio.conns32, at ninety-six. Agreed, 9 of 9.
    provenance: scripts/measure/wal-split-ab.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "2.39x"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md, crates/mira-core/src/wal.rs]

  - key: wal.split.records_per_s_ratio.conns32
    value: 1.039
    unit: ratio
    measures: >
      Records/s with one log per signal over the single-log binary, at thirty-two
      connections. Median of nine per-pass ratios across three sittings minutes
      apart. **Signs split, 6 of 9, so this is not quotable as a speedup** and is
      registered to stop it being re-quoted as one — the first sitting alone
      returned 1.125x on 3 of 3 passes and the other six withdrew it.
    provenance: scripts/measure/wal-split-ab.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "1.039"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md]

  - key: wal.concurrent_appenders.bandwidth_ratio.two
    value: 0.98
    unit: ratio
    measures: >
      Aggregate bytes/s from two threads appending 790 KiB frames to two files on
      the data volume, over one thread doing the same — no Mira code in the loop,
      so it prices the device rather than the engine. Median of five per-pass
      ratios; signs split, 2 of 5. The volume absorbs no second appender, which
      is the mechanism behind wal.split.write_ratio.* and the reason three
      mutexes buy nothing.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "0.98x"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, crates/mira-core/src/wal.rs]

  - key: wal.concurrent_appenders.bandwidth_ratio.three
    value: 0.86
    unit: ratio
    measures: >
      As wal.concurrent_appenders.bandwidth_ratio.two, with three appenders.
      Signs split, 2 of 5.
    provenance: unscripted
    quoted:
      - as: "0.86x"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, crates/mira-core/src/wal.rs]

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # The corpus itself. These are the denominators every query row divides by, so
  # they are measurements too.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - key: corpus.records.logs
    value: 27066368
    unit: records
    measures: >
      Log records in the section 11 corpus, and separately the span count — the
      generator emits one batch of each per round, so the two are equal by
      construction and a difference between them is a bug in the run.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "27,066,368"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md]
      - as: "27.1"
        sites:
          [
            README.md,
            docs/architecture.md,
            docs/market.md,
            docs/agents.md,
            CHANGELOG.md,
          ]

  - key: corpus.records.points
    value: 39648
    unit: points
    measures: >
      Metric data points in the section 11 corpus. Three orders of magnitude
      below the other two signals, which is why a metrics block holds ~1,600
      points and the metrics join's cost is invisible in a mixed run.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "39,648"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md]

  - key: corpus.gib
    value: 8.33
    unit: GiB
    measures: Arrow IPC bytes on disk for the section 11 corpus, sidecars included.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "8.33 GiB"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md]

  - key: corpus.tables
    value: 1652
    unit: tables
    measures: >
      Arrow tables across the corpus — the file count `find -name '*.arrow'`
      returns. Not the block count: a block is a directory of tables, and how
      many depends on how many attribute levels the signal has.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "1,652"
        sites: [docs/architecture.md, docs/market.md]

  - key: corpus.blocks.logs
    value: 137
    unit: blocks
    measures: >
      Log blocks in the section 11 corpus, and the denominator of every
      "N of 137 blocks" in the query table. Six flushers per signal seal ~204,800
      rows each; one flusher sealed 87 blocks of ~330,000 for the same bytes.
    provenance: scripts/measure/conn-sweep.sh
    quoted:
      - as: "137"
        sites:
          [
            README.md,
            docs/architecture.md,
            docs/market.md,
            docs/internals/e2e.md,
            CHANGELOG.md,
          ]
